     Draft Statement on Computer Privacy and Academic
     Freedom(Please note: this is a draft document not a policy
     statement)

                    General Principles

Respect for intellectual labor and creativity is vital to academic
discourse and enterprise.  This principle applies to works of all
authors and publishers in all media.  It encompasses respect for
the right to acknowledgment, right to privacy, and right to
determine the form, manner, and terms of publication and
distribution.  Because electronic information is volatile and
easily reproduced, respect for the work and personal expression of
others is especially critical in computer environments.  Violations
of authorial integrity, includingplagiarism, invasion of privacy,
unauthorized access, and trade secret and copy-right violations,
may be grounds for sanctions against members of the academic
community.(This paragraph is reproduced from "Using Software (A
Guide to the Ethical and Legal Use of Software for Members of the
Academic Community").

                    Relevant Laws and Statutes

           U.S. Copyright Law and Software Licensing
All computer software is protected by the federal copyright
law.Most of the software available in university facilities is
proprietary and is protected by legal licensing agreements in
addition to the copyright law.Special provision for copying may be
granted by the
copyright owner and will be specified within the software package,
documentation, or license agreement.  Inspect these provisions
carefully before making any copies.Under no circumstances should
copyrighted software be distributed outside the university through any
mechanism, electronic or otherwise.Users are responsible for being
aware of the licensing restrictions for the software you use on any
system.  Lack of knowledge does not justify a violation of the
law.Violation of copyright law or licensing agreements may result in
university disciplinary action and/or may result in legal action.

       State of Missouri Law  RSMo 569.093-569.099
Sections 569.093 to 569.099 of the Revised Statutes of Missouri define
three types of illegal computer "tampering":

(1) tampering with computer data,
(2) tampering with computer equipment, and
(3) tampering with computer users.

"Tampering with computer data" includes the following actions taken
without proper authorization:
-modifying or destroying data, programs, or supporting documentation,
whether internal or external to a computer system;
-disclosing or taking data, programs or supporting documentation,
whether internal or external to a computer system;
-disclosing or taking a password, ID, or other information that
controls access to a computer system;
-accessing a computer and intentionally examining information about
another person;-receiving, keeping, using, or disclosing data gained
through one of the actions above.
"Tampering with computer equipment" includes modifying, destroying,
damaging, or taking a computer or device intended to be used with a
computer without proper authorization;
"Tampering with computer users" includes the following actions taken
without proper authorization:
-accessing or causing access to any computer;
-denying or causing the denial of computer system services to an
authorized user.

Criminal penalties are prescribed by Missouri law for all three types
of tampering.
U.S. Federal Law (Electronic Communications Privacy Act
,1986)

This Act gives the same protection to electronic communication that
the "Omnibus Crime Control and Safe Streets Act of 1968,"  had given
to verbal and wire communications.  Specifically, Section 2511(4) (a)
of Title 18 makes unauthorized interception of an electronic
communication a felony with a possible sentence of five years in
prison and a fine of $250,000. The 1986 Act also:

-prohibits unauthorized interception or access to stored wire and
electronic communications;
-prohibits manufacture, distribution, possession and advertising of
devices "primarily useful for surreptitious interception" of wire and
electronic communication;
-extends the 1968 law to protect the privacy of - stored - personal
information and the transmission of such information from one computer
site to another;
-prohibits providers of electronic communication services from
intentionally divulging the contents of a message to anyone other than
the message's intended recipient.

Computer Privacy

Within the framework of the above General Principles it is important
to recognise that the relevant statutes are concerned with the
unauthorized accessing or use of computer files but do not address the
question of the permissibility of authorized but unjustified forms of
such actions. The following paragraphs are therefore included:
It is recognized that computer files may be recorded, stored and
accessed in a variety of physical media and devices, including, for
example, floppy disks, personal computer hard drives, CD-Rom drives
and mainframe library filespace. Insofar as these physical media and
devices are the property of Southeast Missouri State University, the
filespace occupied by a computer file may be regarded as the property
of the university. It should be noted, however, that this does not
imply that the university has ownership of whatever is expressed by or
represented in the documents contained in the computer files occupying
such filespace, be it in the form of theories, data, analyses, ideas,
concepts, artwork, etc. (Indeed, such an implication might lead to
conflicts with the relevant statutes on copyrights or patent rights,
for example.) There is a clear analogy here with a scholarly paper
published in an appropriate journal: the paper might have been
produced on paper owned by the university, using a machine such as a
typewrite, wordprocessor or personal computer which is the property of
the university, but this does not mean that 'the paper', understood as
distinct from the medium in which it was originally produced, is the
property of the university.Furthermore, ownership of such filespace,
in the above sense, does not confer upon the university exclusive
rights of use and access. To introduce another analogy, the ownership
of a rental property, such as a house or apartment, may or may not
have unrestricted access to the property, depending on the terms and
conditions of the lease. Extending this analogy to the present case,
the present document can be thought of as laying down the general
terms of a form of agreement between the university, as 'owner', and
faculty, staff and students, as 'users', concerning the use of and
access to, computer filespace.Thus, the computer filespace of
students, staff, and faculty members, whether it be on floppy disk,
personal computer hard drive, mainframe library, CD-Rom or whatever
other physical medium or device, may not be inspected, copied,
changed, or otherwise tampered with without the express permission of
the user or unless just cause can be given with respect to the
commission of criminal activity as determined by the relevant state
and federal laws. It should be noted that this policy is not concerned
with how much filespace is to be allocated to individual users, nor is
it concerned with the measures to be taken by those responsible for
the administration of the system in response to possible abuse of the
system (the latter should be addressed in a broader policy statement
concerning ethical use in general). In particular it should be
emphasized that a system administrator may have the right to restrict
access to the relevant filespace after determining that abuse has
occurred (such determination to be made with all due process) but,
under this policy, does not have the right to inspect, change or
otherwise tamper with the contents of that filespace.Notice that
copying (i.e., stealing or ''pirating'') computer software is also
prohibited under this policy.

Email Privacy

It is not clear if the Electronic Communications Privacy Act applies
to 'external' email - that is, email generated off-campus, or email
whose destination is off-campus - and thus, whether the Act protects
the privacy of 'internal' email or not. (However, Mike Godwin, legal
services counsel for the Electronic Frontier Foundation believes that
the Act could be reasonably construed to protect university mail.)The
following paragraphs are therefore included to address this issue:
Computer communications systems, networks and bulletin boards promote
the free exchange of ideas and information, thus enhancing teaching
and research. The contents of electronic 'email' files are to be
regarded as private and confidential. Any inspection of such files
and any action based upon such inspection, shall be governed by all
applicable U.S. and Missouri laws and by the general policies of
Southeast Missouri State University concerning computer privacy, as
stated above. Computer users should not use such electronic
communications systems to harass others or in any way interfere with
their work on the computer. (adapted from the Draft Computer Ethics
Statement of the Ad Hoc Committee on Computer Literacy of James
Madison University and the Interim E-Mail Advisory of the University
of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign)

The above considerations are primarily concerned with issues of
privacy in this context. There are, however, related issues concerning
censorship and academic freedom which are dealt with in the
following.

Access to Network News Systems, Bulletin Boards and
Archives

Users of the Southeast Missouri State University central computer
facility (faculty, staff, students) have access to the network news
system, a set of newsgroups concerned with a wide variety of topics.
Members of these groups engage in exchanges of information on matters
of common interest, such as computing, scientific issues, social and
cultural topics, the environment, politics, hobbies, recreational
pursuits, and so forth. This system is the product of a loosely
organized and largely unregulated network which now includes sites
throughout the world. If a site is linked to the network, users can
transmit and receive messages to and from other sites. There is no
central repository for these articles--they are passed automatically
from site to site so that a news article generated at a particular
locale will eventually replicate itself to network-connected news
systems at distant locations.Although the news network has become a
valuable resource for university communities all over the world,
objections are raised from time to time about the content of the
messages and articles. These objections range from complaints that
some of the material is frivolous and unrelated to serious academic
pursuits to complaints that certain sexually-oriented material is
obscene or pornographic. Moreover, there have been instances in which
administrators at other universities have responded to such complaints
by terminating or restricting access to the news network.
Unfortunately, such actions usually have been taken unilaterally, that
is, without consultation with appropriate faculty and student
groups.Censorship of the news network is not presently an issue here
at Southeast Missouri State University nor do we wish to imply that
the administration would act in an arbitrary manner should the issue
arise. Nevertheless, it would clearly be useful to have a policy in
place which would govern access to and participation in the news
network. (adapted from Resolutions Approved by the Computer Policy
Committee, University of Wisconsin-Milwaukee)

The following is intended to express the central principles of such a
policy:

Academic institutions exist for the transmission of knowledge in all
its different forms and are subject to certain principles of
intellectual and academic freedom. Given the close analogies that
exist between print and electronic media, analogous standards
regarding access should be applied to messages, files etc. received
from the news network as are applied to books, journals, documents
etc. held by the university library. In particular, attempts to
remove, block or otherwise censor such material, whether it be in
print or electronic form, run counter to the above
principles.Furthermore, messages and articles posted to the newsgroup
are analogous to publications in print media and should be regarded as
protected by the same standards of intellectual and academic freedom
that apply to the latter. In particular, such messages and articles
posted to the network as originate on this campus should be free from
any form of censorship or prior restraint.Any action by the
administration of Southeast Missouri State University that might
restrict access to the news network should be contemplated only after
consultation with the appropriate committee on computer use, the
Faculty Senate and other appropriate bodies. (based on Resolutions
Approved by the Computer Policy Committee, University of
Wisconsin-Milwaukee)

Users of the Southeast Missouri State University central computer
facility (faculty, staff, students) also have access to a wide variety
of electronic bulletin boards and 'lists'. These differ from
newsgroups in general insofar as articles posted on such bulletin
boards are typically stored at a particular site and are made
available and transmitted only to subscribers to the bulletin board or
list (although 'cross-listing of certain articles between bulletin
boards is not uncommon). Furthermore, the owner of such a bulletin
board or list typically reserves the right to edit the content of
certain articles or to restrict access to the list altogether (within
relevant guidelines set by Federal and state statutes, concerning
anti-discrimination policies for example), if deemed necessary. (It
should also be noted that certain newsgroups are also 'moderated'.)

Within this framework, the same principles concerning access to and
censorship of newgroups, as set down above, should also be taken to
apply to bulletin boards, lists and the articles posted on them.The
same principles concerning access and censorship, as laid down above,
should also be taken to apply to archives, databases, electronic
journals etc., from which users can obtain computer files using
various 'file transfer protocols'. Again, such archives are usually
centrally located and the owner may have the right to edit the content
of, or restrict access to, these files. Violations of these Principles
shall be treated like any other ethical violation as outlined in the
relevant sections of the faculty, staff and student handbooks.

Steven French, Dept. of Philosophy,
Allen Gathman, Dept. of Biology
